da":true,"_score_net_debt_ebitda":100.0,"_gate_accruals":-0.06410293542024353,"_gp_accruals":true,"_score_accruals":57.3,"_gate_rev_volatility":0.39130729922362173,"_gp_rev_volatility":false,"_score_rev_volatility":2.5,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":0.6781653737293165,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":false,"_score_margin_vs_hist":0.0,"_gate_piotroski":5,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":55.6,"_gate_spread":-0.09706620667484613,"_gp_spread":false,"_score_spread":0.0,"_gate_roic_consistency":null,"_gp_roic_consistency":null,"_score_roic_consistency":0.0,"_gate_incr_roic":0.9124533117631096,"_gp_incr_roic":true,"_score_incr_roic":100.0,"_gate_margin_advantage":0.24736385301205288,"_gp_margin_advantage":true,"_score_margin_advantage":100.0,"_gate_pool_share":0.39448011422652796,"_gp_pool_share":true,"_score_pool_share":100.0,"_gate_rev_durability":null,"_gp_rev_durability":null,"_score_rev_durability":0.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":0.6821309724265598,"_gp_fcf_durability":true,"_score_fcf_durability":100.0,"_gate_margins":-0.025708421076146258,"_gp_margins":false,"_score_margins":24.3,"_gate_fund_growth":0.009532170541716,"_gp_fund_growth":false,"_score_fund_growth":9.5,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0023675114065573985,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":13.7,"_gate_share_shrink":0.3181802590302758,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.00927914532738587,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":84.5,"_gate_insider_own":0.00559,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":3.7,"_gate_insider_buying":0.167,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":16.7,"pf":[],"description_full":"Coeur Mining, Inc. operates as a gold and silver producer in the United States, Canada, and Mexico. The company operates through Palmarejo, Rochester, Kensington, Wharf, Silvertip, and Las Chispas segments. It explores for gold, silver, zinc, lead, and other related metals. It markets and sells its concentrates to third-party customers, including refiners and smelters, under off-take agreements. The company was formerly known as Coeur d'Alene Mines Corporation and changed its name to Coeur Mining, Inc. in May 2013. Coeur Mining, Inc. was incorporated in 1928 and is headquartered in Chicago, Illinois.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Mitchell J. Krebs | President, CEO & Chairman | Age 53 | Compensation: $2.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Revenue per employee of $790K ranks in the above the sector median among Basic Materials peers, with revenue growing 10%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $254K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $7K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["Coeur Mining, Inc. is a large-cap basic materials business with a $13.0B market cap on $2.1B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +38% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 1% against a 11% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, and 24% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $666M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 32 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.51\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage of 22\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 0.2%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 2.6\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 5.3% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Weakness in commodity prices pressures materials companies","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Commodity price volatility and cost-curve position \u2014 in a downturn only the lowest-cost producer has a business, and the rest carry the overhead.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Environmental remediation liability \u2014 tightening regulations on emissions, tailings, and water use create open-ended balance-sheet risk that grows with each legislative cycle.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Trade policy and tariff whiplash \u2014 materials producers are first-order casualties of protectionist swings, and margin gets squeezed from both input and output pricing.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 25% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","10 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Growing faster than most Basic Materials peers \u2014 taking share inside the profit pool","Basic Materials sector outperforming the broader market over the past quarter"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"How Is a Larger Portfolio Reshaping Coeur Mining (NYSE:CDE) Results?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/gold/how-is-a-larger-portfolio-reshaping-coeur-mining-nysecde-results","date":"2026-11-26","timestamp":1795